  <h2>The working stack for site analysis and grading</h2>
  <p>For preliminary site work, a small set of general-purpose AI tools covers the majority of the workload:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/tool/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> — drafting site analysis narratives, drainage descriptions, and quantity commentary</li>
    <li><a href="/tool/claude/">Claude</a> — long-context review of geotechnical and survey reports (Pro plan supports 200K tokens, enough for a 100+ page report in one prompt)</li>
    <li><a href="/tool/gemini/">Gemini</a> — structured data work: GIS joins, take-off tables, large CSV exploration</li>
    <li><a href="/tool/dify/">Dify</a> — RAG database over your local stormwater, zoning, and erosion control code so the model can answer with citations</li>
    <li><a href="/tool/n8n/">n8n</a> — automates plan intake, conflict detection, and routing to utility leads</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you are weighing which general LLM to anchor on, our <a href="/vs/chatgpt-vs-claude/">ChatGPT vs Claude</a> comparison and the <a href="/vs/claude-vs-gemini/">Claude vs Gemini</a> breakdown are good starting points.</p>

  <h2>Hydrology, SWPPP, and code lookup</h2>
  <p>The most error-prone use of AI in civil work is letting the model generate numerical answers to hydrology or hydraulics questions. Don't do it. Use AI to <em>set up</em> the calculation, then run it in your solver of record.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Pull your jurisdiction's stormwater manual into <a href="/tool/dify/">Dify</a> as a knowledge base. Chunk by chapter, attach citations, ask specific questions ("Detention sizing for a 2.5-acre residential site in zone B").</li>
    <li>Have <a href="/tool/claude/">Claude</a> draft the SWPPP narrative from the site conditions and your standard details. Verify every numeric claim against the source document.</li>
    <li>Run the actual rainfall-runoff and pipe network sizing in HEC-HMS, EPA SWMM, or your firm's standard tool. AI can help you populate input files from GIS data, but the simulation itself must run in the engineer's tool of record.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For an automation blueprint, see the <a href="/workflows/dify-rag-document-qna/">Dify RAG for document Q&amp;A</a> workflow. Pair it with the <a href="/workflows/n8n-webhook-automation/">n8n webhook automation</a> workflow to push question logs to your project archive.</p>

  <h2>Infrastructure planning and utility coordination</h2>
  <p>AI is at its strongest when the input is messy and the output is a structured coordination action:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use <a href="/tool/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> to draft traffic impact narratives from raw count data</li>
    <li>Use <a href="/tool/gemini/">Gemini</a> to run take-off tables and pipe-network quantity estimates</li>
    <li>Wire <a href="/tool/n8n/">n8n</a> to your plan-review inbox: submitted package → AI extracts utility conflicts → routes to the right discipline lead → tracks the response</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For an adjacent perspective, our <a href="/solutions/ai-for-mep-engineers/">AI for MEP engineers</a> and <a href="/solutions/ai-for-bridge-engineers/">AI for bridge engineers</a> pages cover the infrastructure-adjacent specialties.</p>

  <h2>What AI will not do for you</h2>
  <p>Be explicit about the limits so your team does not over-trust the model:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stamp-bearing work.</strong> Final calculations, sealed reports, and permit sign-offs remain the PE's responsibility under every state board we have seen.</li>
    <li><strong>Numerical hydrology and hydraulics.</strong> Run those in HEC-HMS, SWMM, StormCAD, or similar — never paste numbers back from the LLM without re-running.</li>
    <li><strong>Code citations.</strong> Treat any code paragraph the model produces as a draft. Look up the actual section in your code reference before quoting it in a deliverable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Recommended 30-day rollout</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> Stand up a <a href="/tool/dify/">Dify</a> RAG over your local stormwater and zoning manuals. Pilot on one active project.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> Add <a href="/tool/claude/">Claude</a> for long-report summarisation and <a href="/tool/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> for site-analysis draft text. Lock a "no numbers in chat" rule.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 3:</strong> Wire <a href="/tool/n8n/">n8n</a> into your plan-review intake. Measure conflict-triage time before and after.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Document the firm's working stack, train junior staff, and update standards to call out which AI outputs require a PE's review.</li>
  </ol>
