  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <h3>Can AI handle stormwater design end-to-end?</h3>
  <p>AI can draft narratives, generate calculation templates, and answer code questions when paired with a RAG knowledge base. The actual rainfall-runoff and pipe-network sizing must run in HEC-HMS, EPA SWMM, or your firm's hydrology tool, with results stamped by a licensed PE.</p>

  <h3>What is the best AI tool for civil engineering right now?</h3>
  <p>For drafting and code Q&amp;A, <a href="/tool/claude/">Claude</a> and <a href="/tool/chatgpt/">ChatGPT</a> are the strongest general-purpose options. <a href="/tool/dify/">Dify</a> is the most useful add-on because it lets you ground the model's answers in your actual code references with citations. See our <a href="/solutions/ai-for-civil-engineers/">civil engineering solutions</a> page for the full stack.</p>

  <h3>Is it safe to put client project data into ChatGPT or Claude?</h3>
  <p>Both vendors offer data-opt-out settings on paid plans and Team/Enterprise contracts, but you should still avoid uploading data that is covered by NDA, ITAR, or other confidentiality controls without legal sign-off. For sensitive projects, deploy a self-hosted LLM and your own RAG instead.</p>

  <h3>How accurate is AI for earthwork quantities?</h3>
  <p>AI is useful for early-stage estimates across multiple grading alternatives. Final earthwork quantities should be computed in your civil 3D or OpenRoads model, then compared against the AI estimate as a sanity check — not the other way around.</p>

